{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788361680,"nonce":"osWQzx8Q1FQR0xW6W5NF/ReeRJU=","message":"**Implemented, worker, repair round 2026-09-02.** Branch `fix/f9dadc7-stalled-build`, commit `e732ea2` (rebased onto `main` at `7fbe7db`). Batched with `e08c3e5` on one branch and one vocabulary, per dispatch.\n\n**The issue's headline is UNDERSTATED, and the artifacts are the stronger story.** Checked before implementing, per the round's rule. Measured over all 55 `RUNS/m1-20260901/digests/day-*.json`:\n\n* `awaiting_materials` was **22 for TWENTY consecutive in-game days — days 38 to 57 inclusive**, not fifteen. No gap.\n* **None of those twenty readings was capped.** No `more`, no `cap`, no `cap_note`, with 22-25 elements against a 60-item scan cap. So all twenty were a true CENSUS, not a floor. The number was right; the number was the problem.\n* This is the shape of the whole run, not one incident: a flat 60 across days 13-18, a flat 24 across 21-24 and again 31-36.\n* **The digest directory is not complete and should not be quoted as if it were.** `day-1.json` is a mislabelled day-62-era snapshot (tick 3,719,149, one colonist) and days 2-9, 28, 32 and 35 are absent — 12 of 66 day-boundary digests effectively missing.\n\n`accept/f9dadc7-stalled-build.py --selftest` re-derives all of that from the files, so it cannot be argued away.\n\n**What shipped.** `ConstructionWatch.cs` is `BillWatch`'s shape one surface over: a static dictionary keyed by `thingIDNumber`, sampled from `GameComponentTick` every 2,500 ticks, cleared by `GameBoundary`. No scribed state, per the pre-dispatch resolution and `d16a463`.\n\nItem 1 — every unresolved element publishes six keys, ALWAYS, so an absent one can never be mistaken for a healthy one: `state_since_tick`, `state_age_ticks`, `state_age_days`, `age_basis`, `tracked_since_tick`, `stalled`.\n\n**`stalled` is a TRI-STATE and that is the whole point:**\n\n* `true` — in this state for at least two in-game days\n* `false` — observed ENTERING this state more recently than that\n* `null` — **tracking cannot answer yet**\n\n`null` is never \"clean\". `age_basis` says which kind of age it is: `observed-transition` is exact, `since-first-seen` is a FLOOR (first sight counts nothing — BillWatch's own rule, same argument), `not-tracked` is no measurement at all. A floor OVER the threshold is proof; a floor under it must answer `null`.\n\nItem 2 — `digest.construction` gains `stalled[]` (rows sorted oldest-first, sorted BEFORE the cap so a capped list is the worst six and not an arbitrary six), `stalled_count`, `stalled_more`, `stall_after_ticks`, `tracked_since_tick`, and a `stalled_note` present ONLY while tracking is too young to answer. Each row names `def`, `at`, `state`, `layout_id`, `placement_id`, the age, `age_basis` and `why`.\n\nItem 3 — the material-that-exists distinction is `e08c3e5`'s `no-builder` branch plus the existing `missing[]`/`available` split from `54b0c9a`. One vocabulary, as dispatch required: `state` + a one-sentence `why` (`ConstructionVerbs.Why`), carried identically by `construction`'s items, `digest.construction.stalled[]` and `advance {until:{layout}}`'s `unresolved_items`.\n\nItem 4 — `checklists/turn.md` gains `construction-stalled`, keyed to the day-boundary read, citing `construction.stalled` / `stalled_count` / `tracked_since_tick` by name and teaching that an empty list beside a present `stalled_note` is NOT a clean bill.\n\nItem 5 — `accept/f9dadc7-stalled-build.py`. One command; the orchestrator runs it.\n\n**Three resolutions taken on-issue, all recorded in DESIGN:**\n\n1. **The threshold is elapsed time, not a calendar count.** A half-open window of exactly `2 * GenDate.TicksPerDay` contains at least two multiples of `TicksPerDay`, so `age \u003e= 120000` implies two boundaries crossed and never the reverse — the conservative direction, and no `WorldGrid.LongLatOf` on a hot path. `GenLocalDate.DayOfSeason` (what `digest.time` publishes) is offset from `TicksGame % 60000` by the game's start hour and the tile's longitude, so counting its increments would have needed a per-element longitude lookup to gain nothing.\n2. **The key is the live thing's `thingIDNumber`**, so a blueprint becoming a Frame — or the blueprint `Frame.FailConstruction` puts back — starts a new clock. Both are real state changes (the materials arrived; the work was lost), so losing the age there is correct rather than a limitation. A blueprint nobody ever touches keeps one id for its whole life, which is the case this issue exists for.\n3. **`stalled[]` covers FOUR states, two more than item 2 names.** `awaiting-materials` and `blocked` are the contract; `no-builder` is added because it is exactly this run's Heater and excluding it would drop the headline example out of the headline report; `ready` is added because materials-present-and-nobody-on-it for two days is the README's third triage branch and a genuine stall. `in-progress` is excluded — the clock restarting when a pawn picks the thing up is correct.\n\n**Cost, since this is on `digest`'s hot path.** The sampler goes through `ConstructionVerbs.Probe`, which makes NO `GetStatValueAbstract` call — the state token does not depend on `WorkToBuild`, which is the whole reason it can run inside the tick loop. Display facts (def, cell, layout id, `why`) are cached at first sight, so the digest's roll-up is a dictionary walk with ZERO Verse access, and it therefore covers the sampler's 300-item window rather than the digest's own 60. `Placements.For` and `Layouts.Owning` are both linear scans and are walked once per element, at first sight, never per sample.\n\n**NOT DEMONSTRATED, in those words.** No bench was touched — the worker launches nothing. Offline: 24/24. Specifically not demonstrated: that stall tracking survives a save/load (it does not, by design; what is asserted instead is that a reload is VISIBLE via `tracked_since_tick` and a `null` rather than a `false`); that the age is exact across a state change nobody watched between two samples; and the multi-map `Stalled()` filter.\n\n**Interaction with `bb931b9` (the save verb) and `a1644d6`:** a run that checkpoints and resumes resets stall tracking, which is fine and expected under the resolution, and is stated in `turn.md` and in the envelope's own `stalled_note`. `a1644d6`'s `unenclosed_for` block adopted this issue's *concept* in advance but uses different FIELD NAMES, a different age unit and a two-valued `stale` rather than a tri-state — flagged to the orchestrator, whose call it is which one moves.","files":null}]}